{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1774129287,"nonce":"xck2LqFVYWYPvVzfJCNaJUSufeE=","message":"**Clarification: push vs pull value proposition**\n\nThe channel MCP injected into SDK sessions provides agent-initiated (pull-based) access to Mandible operational layer. Without it, Mandible can only push information into the conversation (via system prompt or user messages). With channel tools, the agent can:\n- `request_work` at natural breakpoints (vs Mandible guessing when to inject)\n- `check_inbox` on its own initiative\n- `report_status` for dashboard visibility\n- `submit_auth` from within the conversation\n\nFor simple single-task sessions this is redundant. For autonomous multi-work-unit agents that self-direct, it is significantly more natural than Mandible scripting a state machine from outside.\n\nThis is Phase 4 — nice-to-have after the core two-mode architecture works.","files":null}]}